evidence.statement:
# 11. What BMC's workflow architecture does establish

BMC's content-package architecture provides:

- case templates;
- task templates;
- email templates;
- notification templates;
- documents;
- processes;
- automated task processes;
- task-flow processes;
- flowsets;
- configuration data.



Therefore the Received Invoice workflow exists inside a broader execution framework capable of combining:

```text
CASE
+
TASKS
+
PROCESS
+
AUTOMATION
+
NOTIFICATION
+
DATA
```

This is stronger implementation intelligence than merely knowing the three task names.

---

# 4. The strongest BMC-specific evidence is actually the workflow engine underneath the task

BMC documents a general approval architecture that can be attached to cases and tasks.

For cases, approval logic can include:

- self-approval;
- approval flows;
- chains of approvals;
- expressions determining which approval applies;
- approval outcomes that determine the next case status.



For tasks, BMC similarly supports:

- self-approval;
- approval flows;
- approval chains;
- approver qualification expressions;
- status transitions after approval/rejection;
- automatic approval of tasks that meet defined expressions.



This gives us a much stronger implementation pattern than the undocumented internal logic of the invoice task.

